1
00:00:02,676 --> 00:00:08,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, what is the purpose, your rules, your precepts?

2
00:00:09,118 --> 00:00:12,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you practice renunciation?

3
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:17,281
[SPEAKER_01]: The purpose of it is developing the strength of your mind.

4
00:00:19,522 --> 00:00:23,023
[SPEAKER_01]: And the absolutely first step for that is to be able to say no to yourself.

5
00:00:25,564 --> 00:00:29,825
[SPEAKER_01]: To say no to your desires, whims, habits.

6
00:00:31,146 --> 00:00:32,206
[SPEAKER_01]: So, every

7
00:00:33,605 --> 00:00:41,791
[SPEAKER_01]: All of the main rules upon which a practice is based is they're very practical, so to speak, functional, necessary in that sense.

8
00:00:42,451 --> 00:00:45,954
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not held because of a belief or because of the authority.

9
00:00:46,675 --> 00:00:54,040
[SPEAKER_01]: They're held because they hold in themselves the practice of meditation and development of the mind.

10
00:00:56,346 --> 00:01:06,335
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, think of the rules as a fertile ground where you can plant seeds of a plant or some trees.

11
00:01:06,535 --> 00:01:15,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not a tree, it's not a seed, but having a grown tree would be impossible if you don't have a fertile ground to do so.

12
00:01:18,846 --> 00:01:23,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, you see the necessity of the fertile ground, you see the necessity of the rules.

13
00:01:25,644 --> 00:01:33,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Without them, there's no point if you're cultivating seeds and watering the plant, when you have destroyed the fertility of the ground, when you have compromised the fundamental rules.

14
00:01:34,272 --> 00:01:35,393
[SPEAKER_00]: But what are you trying to do?

15
00:01:36,314 --> 00:01:36,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

16
00:01:37,155 --> 00:01:37,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

17
00:01:37,415 --> 00:01:38,577
[SPEAKER_00]: What are you trying to grow?

18
00:01:39,097 --> 00:01:42,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're trying to grow the freedom.

19
00:01:43,081 --> 00:01:43,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom of your mind.

20
00:01:43,922 --> 00:01:48,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind is bound by the desire, by the craving, by the feelings.

21
00:01:49,708 --> 00:01:54,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind is liable to feelings, which means when suffering arises, you won't be able to prevent it.

22
00:01:54,669 --> 00:01:56,990
[SPEAKER_01]: When pleasure arises, you won't be able but to indulge it.

23
00:01:58,390 --> 00:01:59,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you're controlled by what you feel.

24
00:02:01,271 --> 00:02:04,292
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're controlled because the mind is too weak in regard to what it feels.

25
00:02:05,292 --> 00:02:07,232
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to become stronger than all those things.

26
00:02:08,233 --> 00:02:14,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And the first step to developing of that strength is mere restraint, being able to say no.

27
00:02:16,054 --> 00:02:17,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to everything, forever,

28
00:02:18,477 --> 00:02:25,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But to certain things that are inseparable from desire, craving, ill-will, hate, anything unwholesome, basically.

29
00:02:30,966 --> 00:02:35,229
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eating at a certain time of the day... Yeah.

30
00:02:35,730 --> 00:02:42,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Eating at a certain time, it's again, it's just like, you eat because it is necessary for the body to remain alive.

31
00:02:43,941 --> 00:02:53,828
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you don't want to be using it as a sensuality, as a form of escape, and then you just keep eating throughout the day, eating only what you like, and so on.

32
00:02:54,288 --> 00:02:59,091
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize a degree of necessity to eat, and that's what you focus on.

33
00:02:59,291 --> 00:03:03,995
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, if you eat enough calories once in a day, that's absolutely fine.

34
00:03:04,635 --> 00:03:05,956
[SPEAKER_01]: There is absolutely no reason to eat more.

35
00:03:07,197 --> 00:03:11,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Eating more than what you need is going to bound to, is going to be bound to come out of sensuality.

36
00:03:12,112 --> 00:03:14,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of craving, out of desire.

37
00:03:18,876 --> 00:03:20,277
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not a life-threatening hunger.

38
00:03:21,297 --> 00:03:24,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, in the evening, you have desire to eat.

39
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:33,725
[SPEAKER_01]: And instead of habitually just giving in to desire and satisfying it by any means, you force yourself to reflect on it.

40
00:03:34,647 --> 00:03:37,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you know you won't eat, you won't break the rule.

41
00:03:37,868 --> 00:03:40,129
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as a form of restraint, what's the alternative?

42
00:03:40,149 --> 00:03:41,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's to think, okay, why?

43
00:03:42,270 --> 00:03:42,570
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

44
00:03:43,351 --> 00:03:44,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I pulled by this desire?

45
00:03:44,631 --> 00:03:46,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is this hunger controlling my mind?

46
00:03:48,053 --> 00:03:51,154
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you... that's how you go beyond the desire, eventually.

47
00:03:51,374 --> 00:03:56,557
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I ate a meal at night, I started eating, then I'll be free from desire.

48
00:03:57,697 --> 00:03:58,157
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you won't.

49
00:03:59,958 --> 00:04:00,959
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be free from hunger.

50
00:04:02,319 --> 00:04:04,040
[SPEAKER_01]: But the hunger was not your desire.

51
00:04:07,155 --> 00:04:11,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there would have been no desire without the hunger, but the hunger is not a desire.

52
00:04:13,579 --> 00:04:14,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire is always mental.

53
00:04:16,661 --> 00:04:23,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the hunger, when the physical state affects, controls your mind, then it becomes a desire.

54
00:04:24,283 --> 00:04:32,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you might go and remove the physical state, but the liability of your mind to be controlled by the physical states remains.

55
00:04:33,029 --> 00:04:37,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, next physical thing comes, and you're gonna equally be bound by the same desire.

56
00:04:38,053 --> 00:04:40,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, you don't set... you can't satisfy a desire.

57
00:04:40,815 --> 00:04:41,755
[SPEAKER_01]: And everybody knows that.

58
00:04:42,456 --> 00:04:47,519
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be chasing and engaging in all sorts of sensuality, you can never satisfy a desire.

59
00:04:47,539 --> 00:04:47,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

60
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:49,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not there where you're trying to satisfy.

61
00:04:50,401 --> 00:04:52,383
[SPEAKER_01]: You're satisfying physical circumstances.

62
00:04:53,318 --> 00:04:55,180
[SPEAKER_01]: But those are not what the desire is.

63
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:58,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, desire is inseparable from those, but it's not those.

64
00:04:59,985 --> 00:05:00,786
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not those things.

65
00:05:02,388 --> 00:05:04,710
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your precepts don't...

66
00:05:05,771 --> 00:05:10,256
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the precepts are basically just like laying out the ground where you can practice.

67
00:05:10,776 --> 00:05:15,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Just putting up a fence, fertilizing the ground and agreeing upon those things.

68
00:05:17,752 --> 00:05:19,413
[SPEAKER_01]: As a starting point, you have to start somewhere.

69
00:05:19,833 --> 00:05:24,957
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all the precepts were basically... That's what I mean, they were quite functional, in that sense.

70
00:05:26,457 --> 00:05:27,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, you eat...

71
00:05:27,438 --> 00:05:29,659
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, the monks were allowed to eat in the evening.

72
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:32,361
[SPEAKER_01]: It was not specified to eat only in the morning.

73
00:05:32,441 --> 00:05:33,442
[SPEAKER_01]: They could eat many times a day.

74
00:05:34,345 --> 00:05:37,706
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, it was sort of becoming impractical for various reasons.

75
00:05:38,506 --> 00:05:44,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Going out at night to collect the food, getting all sorts of accidents and so on.

76
00:05:44,988 --> 00:05:45,989
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and eh...

77
00:05:46,989 --> 00:05:49,170
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then it was sort of, a rule was brought in.

78
00:05:50,010 --> 00:05:51,831
[SPEAKER_01]: You eat it, you eat it at the right time.

79
00:05:52,711 --> 00:05:56,072
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't go out to collect food at night, you go during the day.

80
00:05:56,805 --> 00:05:59,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, they could still eat at night, but then you stop eating at night as well.

81
00:05:59,547 --> 00:06:01,508
[SPEAKER_01]: You just eat when you go and get the food.

82
00:06:01,528 --> 00:06:03,148
[SPEAKER_01]: It just becomes more practical, easier.

83
00:06:03,529 --> 00:06:06,871
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't spend the rest of your day being concerned and planning about the food.

84
00:06:07,251 --> 00:06:11,233
[SPEAKER_01]: You spend it practicing restraint and practicing wisdom and practicing meditation.

85
00:06:12,794 --> 00:06:15,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Like precepts against alcohol.

86
00:06:16,056 --> 00:06:19,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, those, yeah, the eight precepts, those are the basis of practice.

87
00:06:19,517 --> 00:06:21,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are the... that's what defines their fertile ground.

88
00:06:22,919 --> 00:06:23,940
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not negotiable.

89
00:06:25,912 --> 00:06:36,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how can you possibly be practicing the clarity of the mind, developing the strength of your mind, when you're drinking alcohol that's clearly, directly affecting the clarity of the mind?

90
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:38,541
[SPEAKER_01]: And it has no practical value.

91
00:06:39,342 --> 00:06:41,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you need to eat some food.

92
00:06:42,024 --> 00:06:44,605
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to meet a certain degree of... certain amount of calories.

93
00:06:45,006 --> 00:06:46,727
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't need to drink alcohol.

94
00:06:47,367 --> 00:06:49,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, it's always going to be central reasons.

95
00:06:53,998 --> 00:06:58,221
[SPEAKER_01]: And even people say, oh, I drink only because people around me pressure me to do so.

96
00:06:58,282 --> 00:06:59,463
[SPEAKER_01]: That's also a sensual reason.

97
00:07:00,363 --> 00:07:05,287
[SPEAKER_01]: You're basically afraid of affecting adversely the sensual environment around you.

98
00:07:05,527 --> 00:07:06,488
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to upset anyone.

99
00:07:07,009 --> 00:07:11,552
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to have to have to be kind of ostracized or whatever by your friends or not.

100
00:07:11,572 --> 00:07:12,033
[SPEAKER_01]: So you drink.

101
00:07:12,093 --> 00:07:12,713
[SPEAKER_01]: It's ridiculous.

102
00:07:13,194 --> 00:07:19,939
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to develop the strength of your mind, yet you're giving to the basic weaknesses of like, oh, they're laughing and they're teasing me.

103
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:20,620
[SPEAKER_00]: So what?

104
00:07:25,183 --> 00:07:32,740
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you be beyond suffering, beyond the febrile emotion, when someone's mere teasing is affecting you, and you can't... you can't maintain your behavior?

105
00:07:34,784 --> 00:07:35,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Timmy?

106
00:07:53,698 --> 00:07:58,640
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's... precepts aren't... that's not all that you have to do.

107
00:07:59,241 --> 00:07:59,381
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

108
00:07:59,821 --> 00:08:01,502
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a lot more work... Of course.

109
00:08:01,982 --> 00:08:02,802
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a fertile ground.

110
00:08:03,443 --> 00:08:14,168
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have all the fertile ground you want, if you don't have the actual well-cultivated seeds, and if you don't put the work in maintaining that soil, then they're not gonna grow.

111
00:08:16,109 --> 00:08:22,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So being... Because you have to water it, you have to provide enough sun, you have to work around it, once you plant the seeds.

112
00:08:23,750 --> 00:08:29,294
[SPEAKER_01]: But just leaving, just hoping for the soil to do its own, it won't.

113
00:08:29,754 --> 00:08:31,335
[SPEAKER_01]: To do the work, it won't.

114
00:08:31,415 --> 00:08:32,276
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the soil.

115
00:08:32,536 --> 00:08:39,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can have 300 rules, 1000 rules, 5 rules, as many rules as you want.

116
00:08:39,401 --> 00:08:39,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

117
00:08:40,802 --> 00:08:44,385
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter, because there's other work that needs to be done.

118
00:08:45,185 --> 00:08:45,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

119
00:08:45,886 --> 00:08:51,670
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have the rules that cover the necessary basis, and those are basically what is known as eight precepts.

120
00:08:52,565 --> 00:08:55,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Or ten precepts, if you want to take it maybe a step further, but that's it.

121
00:08:56,028 --> 00:08:58,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else is derived from that basis.

122
00:08:58,169 --> 00:08:59,591
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot add more to it.

123
00:09:04,414 --> 00:09:07,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, yeah, having three thousand rules, it's not necessary.

124
00:09:08,317 --> 00:09:15,863
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've covered the basis of these eight precepts, if you don't do anything unwholesome on the account of the eight precepts, that's it.

125
00:09:16,143 --> 00:09:21,808
[SPEAKER_01]: You have sufficient basis for complete freedom from suffering, from feelings and everything else.

126
00:09:22,886 --> 00:09:33,054
[SPEAKER_00]: But what, what, what can you give like a... what's a basic, what's a basic set, precept, that anybody can follow?

127
00:09:34,215 --> 00:09:34,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't...

128
00:09:34,455 --> 00:09:35,977
[SPEAKER_00]: They can establish themselves in virtue.

129
00:09:36,497 --> 00:09:38,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

130
00:09:38,999 --> 00:09:39,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't kill.

131
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:41,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't steal.

132
00:09:42,542 --> 00:09:43,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't cheat.

133
00:09:44,764 --> 00:09:49,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, preferably, or ideally, be a complete celibate.

134
00:09:52,289 --> 00:09:55,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Live a completely celibate lifestyle.

135
00:09:55,811 --> 00:09:58,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't... well, don't...

136
00:10:01,115 --> 00:10:02,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

137
00:10:02,556 --> 00:10:11,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, don't drink and take drugs, in the sense of don't consume things that directly affect mindfulness and awareness and composure.

138
00:10:12,864 --> 00:10:17,027
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to... those are like the five basic ones.

139
00:10:19,527 --> 00:10:22,429
[SPEAKER_01]: And they have to be basically taken seriously.

140
00:10:23,490 --> 00:10:25,331
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm talking about somebody who still lives in the world.

141
00:10:27,132 --> 00:10:28,733
[SPEAKER_01]: For a monk, then you have the eight precepts.

142
00:10:29,053 --> 00:10:39,840
[SPEAKER_01]: You have the non-beautification, non-indulgence in sleep, and not eating at the wrong times.

143
00:10:39,860 --> 00:10:44,082
[SPEAKER_01]: As in not just spend the whole day eating around and thinking about food.

144
00:10:45,083 --> 00:10:46,023
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you wanna start kind of

145
00:10:47,902 --> 00:10:49,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Harnessing your mind, basically.

146
00:10:50,383 --> 00:10:57,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Harnessing the outpours, the behaviors of your mind that are founded upon desire and non-restraint.

147
00:10:58,286 --> 00:11:02,447
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, you're setting up a fence, tying it down, pinning it down until it comes down.

148
00:11:03,107 --> 00:11:04,128
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the precepts are.

149
00:11:05,008 --> 00:11:08,309
[SPEAKER_01]: But in itself, they're not... it's not enough.

150
00:11:09,730 --> 00:11:10,770
[SPEAKER_01]: The necessary basis

151
00:11:12,030 --> 00:11:14,854
[SPEAKER_01]: The container, but then the work needs to be done within that.

152
00:11:15,415 --> 00:11:19,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you fence the yard off for a wild horse, you need to start training it.

153
00:11:20,042 --> 00:11:25,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the first... you can't start training it, inconceivable, when it can always run off into the mountains.

154
00:11:26,506 --> 00:11:28,927
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you fenced it off completely.

155
00:11:28,947 --> 00:11:31,829
[SPEAKER_01]: You repaired any patches that he was escaping through.

156
00:11:32,289 --> 00:11:33,130
[SPEAKER_01]: You brought it back.

157
00:11:33,230 --> 00:11:34,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Now he can't escape.

158
00:11:34,891 --> 00:11:38,092
[SPEAKER_01]: You wait for it to calm down, because he will calm down sooner or later.

159
00:11:38,112 --> 00:11:40,674
[SPEAKER_01]: He's going to get tired running around in circles.

160
00:11:40,714 --> 00:11:42,535
[SPEAKER_01]: When it's calm, then you start training it.

161
00:11:43,655 --> 00:11:47,778
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you remove the fence and you haven't trained it, it's going to run off again.

162
00:11:48,534 --> 00:11:54,199
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to keep the fence until it's fully trained, and when it's fully trained, then it doesn't matter if the fence stays.

163
00:11:55,320 --> 00:11:56,921
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's basically the definition of the precepts.

164
00:11:57,282 --> 00:12:09,653
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even as a layperson, the five precepts are at a certain degree of that... of that fence, which is... which is a good thing, because you are taming down the wild horse, which... what mind is, really, for most people.

165
00:12:10,843 --> 00:12:19,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're taking a bit more control over your own behavior, over your own desires, over your own sort of impulses that usually you can't say no to.

166
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:26,448
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where growth is.

167
00:12:26,468 --> 00:12:28,391
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where development is.

168
00:12:29,473 --> 00:12:30,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the first step.

